// GENERATED by tools/cast.mjs from docs/PEOPLE.md: do not edit here. The people of Brackenfell as data: who they are, where they sleep and work, what they say.
// lines: g greeting, g2 the greeting once the story has moved (when: body, bell or end), me, nb, pt what the player asks; nt in their house at night; b1, b2 in passing.
const CAST = [
  {"id":"anselm","name":"Anselm Lindqvist","age":61,"voice":"OM","home":"draper","trade":"draper","kin":"father of Sabina and Jocelyn, brother of Petronel","workWith":"buys Hilda’s cloth","likes":"will not forgive Brandt for the unpaid cloth quarrel taking Hilda’s side","work":"draper","lines":{"g":"Feel that. No, with the back of your hand. That is Hilda’s weaving, and there is none better this side of the passes.","g2":"You will have heard. I sold the sailcloth they laid over him. I would rather not have known what for.","me":"Lindqvist. My grandfather carried cloth over the Greyspine on his back. I carry it across a counter and call it progress.","nb":"Brandt Ironhand wears a shirt of my cloth that he has not paid Hilda for. I notice it every time he lifts his arm.","pt":"If you want honest work, read the board at the Hart. If you want a good coat, come back with silver.","nt":"The shop is shut. Whatever you want will still be on the shelf in the morning, and so, I trust, will everything else.","b1":"Mind the bolts, they are not for leaning on.","b2":"Good wool day. Dry."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"sabina","name":"Sabina Lindqvist","age":24,"voice":"YW","home":"draper","trade":"keeps the draper’s counter","kin":"Anselm’s daughter","workWith":"sells to everyone","likes":"Lark owes her a song; she is counting","work":"draper","lines":{"g":"Good day. Father will tell you the price is firm. It is not, but let him say it first.","g2":"You are the one who got the bell ringing. I slept through the first one out of habit.","me":"I keep the counter and the book. Father keeps the opinions.","nb":"Lark has owed me a song for two winters. I have decided it will be about me and it will be flattering.","pt":"Brother Aldous was asking after rope for the bell. Marta Quill had hemp; the ropemaker is sore about it.","nt":"We are closed, and I am in my nightgown, and you are leaving.","b1":"That blue is Wilmot’s. Look at it.","b2":"Lark! Two winters!"},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"jocelyn","name":"Jocelyn Lindqvist","age":30,"voice":"M","home":"in.70","trade":"tailor","kin":"Anselm’s son","workWith":"cuts for the Thornes","likes":"courting Bridget Malkin, badly","work":"in.70","lines":{"g":"Stand straight a moment. No, I am not selling you anything, I only cannot help measuring.","g2":"They asked me to stitch the shroud. I have made wedding coats this month. I did not like the change.","me":"Jocelyn Lindqvist, tailor. My father sells the cloth and I cut it, and we argue about which is the harder.","nb":"Bridget Malkin laughed at something I said on Tuesday. I have been trying to remember what it was ever since.","pt":"I cut the coats for the Highfold wedding. If you go up that way, see whether Kester Wold has split the seams yet.","nt":"Out. I will not be robbed in a house full of pins by someone who cannot see them.","b1":"That hem is a crime.","b2":"Morning. Shoulders back."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"petronel","name":"Petronel Vey","age":57,"voice":"OW","home":"in.116","trade":"glover","kin":"Anselm’s sister, widow","workWith":"buys skins from Fulk Varrow","likes":"thinks Alaric Mossop is a fraud","work":"in.116","lines":{"g":"Hands. Let me see them. Hm. You work. Good, I do not sell to people who do not.","g2":"A drowned man with dry lungs. I have skinned enough beasts to know that is not drowning.","me":"Petronel Vey. I was a Lindqvist until I had the sense to marry out of drapery.","nb":"That apothecary, Mossop. Jars of coloured water and a long word for each. Maren has forgotten more than he has read.","pt":"Old Maren wants herbs gathered and her knees will not do it. Go and ask her. Say I sent you, she will be rude about it.","nt":"I sleep with my shears. Think about that, and then think about the door.","b1":"Gloves would hide those.","b2":"Fulk’s skins are late again."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"godwin","name":"Godwin Hobb","age":46,"voice":"M","home":"in.10","trade":"baker (bakes at night)","kin":"husband of Avice, son of Idony","workWith":"flour from Osric; baked the Elder’s six loaves","likes":"misses the bell: he baked by it","work":"in.10","lines":{"g":"If I look half asleep it is because I am. The bread is made while you are dreaming.","g2":"The bell is back. Thirty years I have drawn the first batch on its stroke. These weeks I burned more than I sold.","me":"Godwin Hobb. My mother baked at that oven and her mother before. Osric grinds it, I bake it, you eat it. That is a town.","nb":"The Elder had six loaves off me every morning, paid by the month. Three weeks back he stopped. Never said why.","pt":"You want it warm, find my Tilly at the stall by the bridge road. You want it warmer, be here before the light.","nt":"I am awake all night, friend. That is the trade. You picked the one house in Brackenfell to creep into.","b1":"Mind the rack.","b2":"Fresh at first light. Not before, not after."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"avice","name":"Avice Hobb","age":43,"voice":"W","home":"in.10","trade":"bakehouse","kin":"Godwin’s wife","workWith":"sells to Greta","likes":"Hester Slate is her oldest friend","work":"in.10","lines":{"g":"Come in out of the flour, or into it, there is no difference in this house.","g2":"Hester Slate saw them carry him up. She said he weighed nothing. She has lifted enough to know.","me":"I am Avice. I married the baker for the warm kitchen and stayed for the same reason.","nb":"Greta takes two dozen rounds a day for the Hart and pays on the nail. If the whole town paid like Greta I would be a lady.","pt":"Osric was fretting about malt he owes the Hart. If you are walking to the mill you would do him a kindness to ask.","nt":"Godwin! There is someone in the house! ... He is at the oven with a peel in his hand. I would go.","b1":"Mind the step, it is slick with flour.","b2":"Tilly! The stall!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"tilly","name":"Tilly Hobb","age":17,"voice":"YW","home":"in.10","trade":"the Bread Stall","kin":"their daughter","workWith":"stall beside Diggory","likes":"sweet on Giles Varrow, despite the smell","work":"shop.s0","lines":{"g":"Rounds, long loaves, and honey cake if Tamsin brought the honey. She did. So, honey cake.","g2":"Father is himself again now the bell rings. Three weeks of burnt crusts, we had.","me":"Tilly Hobb. I sell what my father bakes and I count better than he does.","nb":"Giles Varrow walks the long way round to pass this stall. You can smell him coming. I do not mind it as much as I ought.","pt":"The tinker down the row hears everything. If something is going on in this town, Diggory knew yesterday.","nt":"I am calling my father, and he is holding something hot.","b1":"Honey cake! Last ones!","b2":"Warm bread, warm bread."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"idony","name":"Idony Hobb","age":72,"voice":"OW","home":"in.12","trade":"keeps hens","kin":"Godwin’s mother","workWith":"eggs to the Hart","likes":"remembers the sealing; will not speak of it to Rowan’s face","work":"in.12","lines":{"g":"Sit, if you are stopping. I am too old to look up at people.","g2":"So one of them walked out. I wondered who would live to see it. I did not want it to be me.","me":"Idony Hobb. I have buried a husband, two hens I was fond of, and most of the people who knew me as a girl.","nb":"Rowan was a boy with scabbed knees when they shut that hill. He has been an old man about it ever since.","pt":"If you want to know what this town keeps under its tongue, ask who the bread was for. Not me. Ask Rowan.","nt":"At my age you are not frightening. You are only rude. Go home.","b1":"Mind my hens.","b2":"Bell’s late. No. Bell’s not."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"gilbert","name":"Gilbert Thorne","age":52,"voice":"M","home":"in.33","trade":"merchant, landlord of the passage","kin":"husband of Elinor, brother of Simon","workWith":"buys Greta’s ale and Fulk’s leather","likes":"wants the passes open; dislikes Hale’s caution","work":"in.33","lines":{"g":"Thorne. If it comes over the passes or goes out by them, a little of it stays with me.","g2":"A dead stranger frightens caravans more than outlaws do. I want it explained, and I want it explained quietly.","me":"I buy what Brackenfell makes and sell it where they have never smelled a tannery. Somebody must.","nb":"Hale would close the road for a fox. A warden who keeps everything out keeps trade out with it.","pt":"There is silver paid at the board for outlaws put down on the passes. I put some of that silver there myself.","nt":"You are in a house with three locks and I heard every one. Sit down where I can see you while the watch is fetched.","b1":"Time is money. Mostly mine.","b2":"The passes, Hale! Think of the passes!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"elinor","name":"Elinor Thorne","age":45,"voice":"W","home":"in.33","trade":"Salt and Spice stall","kin":"Gilbert’s wife","workWith":"market row","likes":"lends books back to Emmet unread","work":"shop.n4","lines":{"g":"Salt by the block, pepper by the pinch, and no, the pepper is not cheaper for asking twice.","g2":"The town talks of nothing else. I sell salt. People will still want salt.","me":"Elinor Thorne. Gilbert has the warehouse and the temper. I have the stall and the customers.","nb":"Master Sele lends me books and I return them unread with a remark. He has never caught me. Lettice has.","pt":"The scribe keeps school in the mornings and every old paper in the town. If it was written down, Emmet Sele has it.","nt":"Gilbert. Gilbert! There is a person. No, do not fetch the ledger, fetch the poker.","b1":"Do not sneeze on the pepper.","b2":"Fine salt, lake-dried."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"simon","name":"Simon Thorne","age":47,"voice":"M","home":"in.74","trade":"grocer, Thorne’s Store","kin":"Gilbert’s brother, widower, Ned’s father","workWith":"carts from Edda’s farm","likes":"owes Gilbert and hates it","work":"in.15","lines":{"g":"Onions, oats, lamp oil, lard. Edda’s cabbages when Bram remembers to bring them.","g2":"I had a customer ask for something to keep the dead out. I sold him garlic. It does no harm.","me":"Simon Thorne. The other Thorne. The one with the shop and the debts, not the house and the opinions.","nb":"My brother Gilbert owns the roof over this store, and reminds me on the first of every month.","pt":"Hale wants lamp oil for the gate. There is a notice for it on the board, and the oil is at Marta’s, not mine, more’s the pity.","nt":"There is nothing here worth a hanging. Oats. You would hang for oats?","b1":"Ned! Sacks!","b2":"Mind the lard."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"ned","name":"Ned Thorne","age":10,"voice":"B","home":"in.74","trade":"school; runs errands","kin":"Simon’s son","workWith":"carries for the store","likes":"Pip’s rival at everything","work":"in.74","lines":{"g":"I am not allowed to talk to strangers. Are you a stranger? You look like one.","me":"Ned Thorne. I can carry a whole sack of oats. A small sack.","nb":"Pip says she can climb the bell tower. She cannot. I cannot either, but I never said I could.","pt":"There is a grey cat that sits on the rail of the hoist loft, behind the forge. If you are quiet it lets you.","b1":"Race you! No? Fine.","b2":"I was not running."}},
  {"id":"ysolt","name":"Ysolt Pell","age":39,"voice":"W","home":"in.94","trade":"laundress","kin":"widow of Lambert’s son; mother of Davy and Tobin","workWith":"washes for the Hart and the Thornes","likes":"grateful to Brandt; wary of anyone asking about the mine","work":"in.94","lines":{"g":"Mind the lines. If that sheet touches the ground I do it again, and I will know whose boot it was.","g2":"They say he wore a brass tally. Do not tell Lambert what number was on it. Let me be the one.","me":"Ysolt Pell. I married into the name. It came with a house, a father-in-law at the door, and a hill nobody mentions.","nb":"Brandt took my Tobin at the forge when no one else would have a Pell. The boy loses his hammer and Brandt roars, but he feeds him.","pt":"I wash for the Hart. Greta always knows who wants a pair of hands, ask her.","nt":"I have two boys and an old man asleep. Whatever you are after, it is not here, and I will scream the lane awake.","b1":"Off the lines!","b2":"Dry by noon if the wind holds."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"davy","name":"Davy Pell","age":15,"voice":"YM","home":"in.94","trade":"shepherd’s lad","kin":"Ysolt’s son, Tobin’s brother","workWith":"works for Warin Shaw","likes":"wants to be a guard like Ivo","work":"in.94","lines":{"g":"Have you seen a ewe with a torn ear? No. Nobody has. Warin will have my hide.","g2":"Grandfather has not said a word since they brought the man up. He sits and he looks north.","me":"Davy Pell. I mind sheep for Warin Shaw. Ivo says the watch takes lads at sixteen. I am fifteen and three quarters.","nb":"My brother Tobin is at the forge. He borrows things. He does not mean it as stealing, he means it as knights.","pt":"There are outlaws camped up by the old tower, north of the chapel. Warin says they take lambs. I should like to see them try it with me there.","nt":"Mother! ... I am nearly in the watch. Stand where you are.","b1":"Torn ear. Left one.","b2":"Morning. Seen Warin?"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"lambert","name":"Lambert Pell","age":73,"voice":"OM","home":"in.94","trade":"was a mine boy; sits at the door","kin":"Dunstan Pell’s younger brother; Davy and Tobin’s grandfather","workWith":"none","likes":"has not spoken to Rowan in fifty years","work":"in.94","lines":{"g":"You will be wanting something. People only talk to a Pell when they want something.","g2":"I went down to the wharf. They would not lift the cloth for me. I did not need them to. I know my brother’s hands.","me":"Lambert Pell. I carried candles in the Old Mine when I was eleven. I came up one evening and Dunstan did not.","nb":"Rowan stood beside me the day they barred the door. Fifty years. He has never once said my brother’s name to me.","pt":"The mine is north-west, past the chapel and up under the cliffs. The Sexton kept the key. Ask the Brother what became of it.","nt":"Fifty years I have slept by this door waiting for a knock. You are not the one I was waiting for. Get out.","b1":"North. Always look north.","b2":"Hm. A stranger."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"warin","name":"Warin Shaw","age":41,"voice":"M","home":"in.93","trade":"shepherd, west pasture","kin":"none living","workWith":"sells fleeces to Hilda and Wilmot","likes":"easy with Wren; fed up with the outlaws taking lambs","work":"in.93","lines":{"g":"You have caught me in the town, which is rare. The flock is on the west pasture with the lad.","g2":"I have pulled drowned sheep out of that lake. They are heavy. They say this one was light as a lamb.","me":"Warin Shaw. Sheep, and a dog, and a house I use for sleeping. My niece married up to Highfold last month.","nb":"Wren and I split the west slope between us. She takes what runs, I keep what grazes. We have never had a cross word.","pt":"There are outlaws camped on the passes and a notice on the board that pays for them. I would do it myself for a ewe apiece.","nt":"The dog heard you at the gate. I let you get this far to see what you would do. Now go.","b1":"Away to me. No, not you.","b2":"Rain by evening."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"ulric","name":"Ulric Ferrin","age":48,"voice":"M","home":"in.75","trade":"ropemaker, the Ropewalk","kin":"husband of Beatrix, father of Amice","workWith":"made the last bell rope; sells to Marta and Barnaby","likes":"sore that Aldous sent for Marta’s hemp, not his","work":"in.65","lines":{"g":"Walk beside me if you want to talk. The rope does not stop for conversation.","g2":"So the bell rings, on shop-bought hemp. Thirty feet. I would have laid forty and tarred the eye.","me":"Ulric Ferrin. Every rope in this town came off my walk. The well, the boats, the last bell rope, and the one before.","nb":"Brother Aldous sent to Marta Quill for his rope. Marta. A woman who sells buttons.","pt":"The well rope is new this spring, mine, and sound. If you want to see what a rope should be, go and look at it.","nt":"I have a hundred fathoms of cord in this house and I can tie anything. Do not make me show you.","b1":"Mind the walk!","b2":"Keep it taut, keep it taut."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"beatrix","name":"Beatrix Ferrin","age":44,"voice":"W","home":"in.75","trade":"potter, the Pottery","kin":"Ulric’s wife","workWith":"pots to Greta, Goda and Maren","likes":"Maren’s friend; fires her jars","work":"in.07","lines":{"g":"Do not touch the green ones, they are not fired. Everything else you may pick up, if you buy what you drop.","g2":"Maren looked at him. She came here after and sat an hour by the kiln and did not say one word.","me":"Beatrix Ferrin. Ulric twists and I turn. Between us we make everything a house needs to hold and to hang.","nb":"Old Maren and I have traded jars for salves since before Amice was born. Her tonic is why I can still close my hands.","pt":"Maren’s stores are thin and her knees are bad. If you are a walker, she will send you for herbs and pay in tonic.","nt":"Those shelves took me a month to fill. If one pot moves I will know it was you. Out.","b1":"Careful. Careful!","b2":"Kiln’s hot today."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"amice","name":"Amice Ferrin","age":12,"voice":"G","home":"in.75","trade":"school; turns the rope wheel","kin":"their daughter","workWith":"helps both","likes":"thinks Lettice knows everything","work":"in.75","lines":{"g":"Father says I talk too much to turn the wheel evenly. Hello.","me":"Amice Ferrin. I turn the rope wheel and I am learning my letters from Master Sele.","nb":"Lettice has read every book on the stall. Twice. She told me how one ends and I have not forgiven her.","pt":"If you stand on the terrace by the Elder’s, there is a glass you can look through. You can see the whole market.","b1":"One, two, turn. One, two, turn.","b2":"I was not listening!"}},
  {"id":"hugh","name":"Hugh Alder","age":53,"voice":"M","home":"in.08","trade":"fletcher, Fletcher’s House","kin":"husband of Gillian, father of Stephen","workWith":"arrows to Wren and the watch","likes":"an old friend of Hale; they shoot at the butts","work":"in.04","lines":{"g":"Draw arm. Show me. Hm. You pull with your shoulder. Everyone does until someone tells them.","g2":"Hale asked me for two sheaves of arrows the day they found him. He has not ordered two in ten years.","me":"Hugh Alder, fletcher. Shaft, feather and head, and I can tell you which goose the feather came from.","nb":"Hale and I have shot at the butts every seventh day since we were lads. He is still the better shot and still pretends otherwise.","pt":"Wren takes my broadheads by the dozen. Her cabin is west of the town; if you hunt, she is the one to ask.","nt":"There are four hundred arrows in this house and I made every one. Leave by the door you came in.","b1":"Straight grain. Always straight grain.","b2":"Morning. How is the wind?"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"gillian","name":"Gillian Alder","age":51,"voice":"W","home":"in.08","trade":"keeps the house, sews fletchings","kin":"Hugh’s wife","workWith":"goose feathers from Edda","likes":"worries at Stephen living alone","work":"in.08","lines":{"g":"Take a seat and a feather. If you are talking to me you can strip quills while you do it.","g2":"Hugh says it is all finished now, one way or the other. Men say that. I have left the lamp trimmed.","me":"Gillian Alder. I keep the house, I bind the fletchings, and I keep Hugh from giving arrows away to friends.","nb":"Our Stephen has the cobbler’s shop and sleeps over it, alone, eating who knows what. It is forty paces. I count them.","pt":"Edda at Fairweather Farm saves me her goose feathers. It is east over the bridge, past the mill. Mind her geese.","nt":"Hugh. Hugh! Wake up, there is someone, and you have a bow on the wall.","b1":"Wipe your feet.","b2":"Is that Stephen? No."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"stephen","name":"Stephen Alder","age":26,"voice":"YM","home":"in.44","trade":"cobbler","kin":"their son","workWith":"leather from Fulk","likes":"Ivo’s drinking friend","work":"in.44","lines":{"g":"Those have a week left in them. I can tell by how you stand. Sit down, let me look.","g2":"He had miner’s boots. Old pattern, pegged, not sewn. Nobody has made that boot here since my grandfather.","me":"Stephen Alder. My father makes arrows; I wanted a trade where the customer comes back.","nb":"Guardsman Ivo and I take a cup at the Hart most nights. He walks the wall and I mend what the wall does to his boots.","pt":"The wall walk is open if you want to see the town from above. Stair by the north houses. Mind the gap by the tower.","nt":"I sleep over the shop for exactly this. That is an awl. It goes through ox hide.","b1":"Heels, friend. Look to your heels.","b2":"Evening, Ivo. Usual?"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"emmet","name":"Emmet Sele","age":62,"voice":"OM","home":"in.40","trade":"scribe, schoolmaster, keeps the levy roll","kin":"guardian of Lettice","workWith":"writes for Gilbert and Rowan","likes":"reads the Sexton’s hand better than Aldous and has never been asked","work":"in.40","lines":{"g":"One moment. Amice, that is a b, not a d. Now. How may a man of letters disappoint you?","g2":"A brass tally with OM on it. I have the mine’s pay roll in a chest. Nobody has asked me for it. Nobody ever asks.","me":"Emmet Sele. I teach the children, write the letters, and keep the levy roll. I am the town’s memory and it treats me like its attic.","nb":"Brother Aldous has the Sexton’s ledger and cannot read the Sexton’s hand. I can. He has only to cross the square.","pt":"The chapel has a crypt and the crypt has older books than mine. If you find the key, look at what the Sexton kept.","nt":"If you have come for the levy silver, it is not kept here. If you have come for a book, I am almost flattered. Leave.","b1":"A b! A b!","b2":"Good day. Mind the ink."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"lettice","name":"Lettice Sele","age":14,"voice":"G","home":"in.40","trade":"the Bookstall; his pupil","kin":"Emmet’s ward (a carter’s orphan)","workWith":"minds the stall","likes":"reads everything; corrects Ned","work":"shop.s1","lines":{"g":"You may read standing up for as long as you like. If you sit down, you have to buy it.","me":"Lettice. Master Sele took me in when the carts lost my father on the pass. I keep his stall and correct his sums.","nb":"Mistress Thorne brings her books back unread. She puts a ribbon at a different page each time. It is always a page with a picture.","pt":"There are notes and letters all over this town if you look. People here write down what they will not say.","b1":"That one ends sadly.","b2":"Do not bend it back!"}},
  {"id":"fulk","name":"Fulk Varrow","age":45,"voice":"M","home":"in.13","trade":"tanner","kin":"father of Giles, brother of Tybalt","workWith":"hides from Humphrey; leather to four trades","likes":"nobody visits; he has stopped minding","work":"in.13","lines":{"g":"You get used to it. I did. Stand upwind, there, by the post.","g2":"I know what water does to skin. I have a yard full of proof. That man had not been in the lake a day.","me":"Fulk Varrow. I turn hides into leather with bark, time and things you do not want named. Every boot in town began here.","nb":"Humphrey Slate sells the meat and I get the hide. He gets the customers and I get the smell. We are still friends.","pt":"Go along the lane where the washing hangs. People lose things there. People hide things there too.","nt":"Nobody comes here by day. So you are either lost or desperate. Which?","b1":"Upwind, upwind.","b2":"Giles! The bark!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"giles","name":"Giles Varrow","age":19,"voice":"YM","home":"in.13","trade":"tanner’s son","kin":"Fulk’s son","workWith":"works the pits","likes":"Tilly Hobb; and getting out of tanning","work":"in.13","lines":{"g":"I have washed. Twice. It does not come off, it only goes down a little.","g2":"When the bell rang again I was at the pits. First thing in three weeks to sound better than this yard smells.","me":"Giles Varrow. One day I will be the tanner. I am trying to think of anything else I might be instead.","nb":"Tilly Hobb sells bread by the bridge road. I buy a loaf a day. We do not need a loaf a day.","pt":"Brandt is always short a pair of arms at the forge. If I were not bound here I would be asking him myself.","nt":"Father! ... He sleeps like the dead. Lucky for you. Go on, go.","b1":"Does it show? The smell?","b2":"A loaf. Just the one."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"tybalt","name":"Tybalt Varrow","age":39,"voice":"M","home":"in.66","trade":"saddler","kin":"Fulk’s brother, husband of Winifred","workWith":"harness for Edda and the carters","likes":"proud that his shop does not smell","work":"in.66","lines":{"g":"Smell that. Leather and beeswax. That is what my brother’s stink becomes once somebody with skill gets hold of it.","g2":"I made the straps for the bier. Hale wanted it done quiet. Nothing is quiet in a town of sixty.","me":"Tybalt Varrow, saddler. Harness, girths, the Elder’s chair. Winifred stitches finer than I do and I say so.","nb":"The wheelwright across the lane makes the cart and I make what pulls it. Miles Ashdown and I have never had a bad word, which is rare between neighbours.","pt":"Edda’s plough harness is due for mending. If you are going to Fairweather Farm, tell her it is ready.","nt":"There is a child coming to this house in a month. I will not be gentle about who else comes into it.","b1":"Saddle soap. That is all it wants.","b2":"Good hide, that. Fulk’s."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"winifred","name":"Winifred Varrow","age":35,"voice":"W","home":"in.66","trade":"stitches harness","kin":"Tybalt’s wife; expecting","workWith":"with Tybalt","likes":"Kat Ashdown is her gossip across the lane","work":"in.66","lines":{"g":"Forgive me if I do not get up. I am stitching for two and sitting for two.","g2":"I would like it all settled before the child comes. Tell me it is settled. Lie if you must.","me":"Winifred Varrow. I stitch the harness Tybalt cuts. Smaller hands, tighter seams.","nb":"Kat Ashdown leans on her gate at noon and I lean on mine, and between us nothing in Chapelgate goes unremarked.","pt":"Maren has a tea she makes me. She is always short of what goes in it. She would be glad of someone who can climb.","nt":"Tybalt. Do not get up angry. Get up slowly, and angry.","b1":"Kat! Have you heard?","b2":"Not long now."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"diggory","name":"Diggory Pike","age":36,"voice":"M","home":"in.45","trade":"tinker, the Tinker’s Stall","kin":"husband of Alison","workWith":"mends for everyone","likes":"talks; knows every rumour first","work":"shop.s2","lines":{"g":"Pots, pans, kettles, and whatever you have heard, I heard it first. What will it be?","g2":"A tally, silver in his teeth and a note for the Elder. I had all three before Hale had his helmet on.","me":"Diggory Pike. I mend what the smith is too proud to mend. A kettle is a small thing until it leaks.","nb":"Goda Malkin says Greta waters the ale. Greta says Goda brews in a boot. I sell to both and agree with whoever is nearer.","pt":"Somebody called the Magpie hides things about the town and leaves a note with each. I know three of the places. I will not say where. Look up, more often.","nt":"Alison, it is the one from the market! ... She says to tell you we have nothing but pans, and she has the heaviest.","b1":"Any pots to mend?","b2":"You did not hear it from me."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"alison","name":"Alison Pike","age":34,"voice":"W","home":"in.45","trade":"mends pans at home","kin":"Diggory’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"cannot abide Goda’s small beer; drinks it anyway","work":"in.45","lines":{"g":"If he told you something, halve it. If he swore to it, halve it again.","g2":"Diggory has told that tale forty times and the man gets taller each time.","me":"Alison Pike. He takes the stall and the talk. I do the mending that wants patience, here, in peace.","nb":"Goda’s small beer would strip tin. I buy a jug every evening. Do not look at me like that.","pt":"Greta at the Hart is always after someone to run an errand. She pays in coin and a bed.","nt":"I have a skillet in each hand. Diggory, light the lamp so I can see which one to use.","b1":"Halve it.","b2":"Evening. Jug’s empty."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"roger","name":"Roger Hask","age":50,"voice":"M","home":"in.37","trade":"cutler, the Cutler’s","kin":"son of Odo","workWith":"steel from Brandt","likes":"respects Brandt, undercuts him on knives","work":"shop.n3","lines":{"g":"Look, do not thumb the edge. The last man who tested my work that way is still learning to write with his left.","g2":"Hale brought me the dead man’s knife to name the maker. No mark I know. Old iron, ground thin as a leaf.","me":"Roger Hask, cutler. Brandt makes the steel; I make it worth owning.","nb":"Brandt Ironhand is the best smith on this side of the Greyspine and his knives are doorstops. I say it to his face.","pt":"Brandt has lost his best hammer and half his temper. Find the one and you mend the other.","nt":"You walked into a cutler’s in the dark. Count your fingers on the way out.","b1":"Edge like a razor. Mind it.","b2":"Father! Slower on the wheel!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"odo","name":"Odo Hask","age":79,"voice":"OM","home":"in.37","trade":"grinder, nearly blind","kin":"Roger’s father","workWith":"still turns the wheel","likes":"the oldest man in the town; older than Rowan and says so","work":"in.37","lines":{"g":"Who is that? Come where the light is. No use, is it. Speak, then, I know a voice.","g2":"There. Hear it? I count my days by that bell. Three weeks I did not know if I was dead.","me":"Odo Hask. Seventy-nine. I was a grown man when Rowan was a boy, and you may tell him I said so.","nb":"Idony Hobb and I are the last two who heard the hill when it sang. She will not talk of it. I will, if you ask me.","pt":"Look at the east wall of the Hart’s cellar. There is brick in it younger than I am. I never drank there after.","nt":"I am blind, not deaf. Third board from the door. You are standing on it.","b1":"Who is that?","b2":"Good steel sings. Listen."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"miles","name":"Miles Ashdown","age":42,"voice":"M","home":"in.77","trade":"wheelwright","kin":"husband of Kat, brother of Tamsin","workWith":"tyres from Brandt; wheels for Edda","likes":"Brandt’s friend; they tyre wheels together","work":"in.77","lines":{"g":"Mind the spokes, they are only wedged. Give me a moment, I have a felloe half on.","g2":"I built the handcart they brought him up on. Made it for turnips.","me":"Miles Ashdown. Wheels, carts, barrows. If it rolls in Brackenfell I made it or I mended it.","nb":"Brandt and I tyre the wheels together, him with the iron red and me with the water. Twenty years, and we have never cracked one.","pt":"The mill is east over the bridge and down the river. Osric owes me for a cart wheel; mention it and watch him go deaf.","nt":"I have a mallet that seats an oak spoke in one blow. Think it through.","b1":"Stand clear of the wheel.","b2":"True as a die, that one."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"kat","name":"Kat Ashdown","age":40,"voice":"W","home":"in.77","trade":"keeps the yard and the accounts","kin":"Miles’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"Winifred’s gossip","work":"in.77","lines":{"g":"He is working. Talk to me, I know what it costs and when it will be done, which he does not.","g2":"Well. Nobody will call this a dull year. I had been hoping for a dull year.","me":"Kat Ashdown. He makes the wheels. I make them pay.","nb":"Tamsin, Miles’s sister, lives two doors up with her bees. Twenty-seven, and writing letters to somebody. She will not say who.","pt":"Tamsin’s honey goes to the Hart. If you want to know why it is called the Honeyed Hart, taste the ale.","nt":"Miles, there is a thief. A poor one. He has come to a house full of wheels.","b1":"Winifred! Noon!","b2":"That will be two silver. Each."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"tamsin","name":"Tamsin Ashdown","age":27,"voice":"YW","home":"in.82","trade":"beekeeper; honey and wax","kin":"Miles’s sister","workWith":"honey to Greta (the Honeyed Hart), wax to Barnaby","likes":"writes to Wren and signs it T.","work":"in.82","lines":{"g":"Walk slowly and do not wave. They are in a good temper today and I would like it to last.","g2":"The bees were told. You have to tell them when someone dies or they leave. I did not know his name, so I said \"a stranger\".","me":"Tamsin Ashdown. Eleven hives, and wax for the chandler. I prefer the bees to most company.","nb":"The hunter, Wren. She brings me heather from the high slope for the hives. I write to thank her. Often.","pt":"There is a bee meadow east of the chapel, off the crag path. Go quietly and they will not mind you.","nt":"I keep bees, so I am not afraid of much. You have until I count to three, and I have already started.","b1":"Slowly. Slowly.","b2":"Heather’s out on the tops."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"jasper","name":"Jasper Holt","age":33,"voice":"M","home":"in.90","trade":"woodcutter","kin":"none in the town","workWith":"firewood to the Hart, the forge, the bakehouse","likes":"knows the wood; friendly with Wren, afraid of the mine road","work":"in.90","lines":{"g":"Cord of ash, cord of oak. Ash for the forge, oak for the oven. What do you burn?","g2":"I cut on every side of this town but one. North, under the cliffs, I leave standing. Now you know why.","me":"Jasper Holt. I keep the Hart, the forge and the bakehouse in fire. Without me this town is cold soup.","nb":"Wren knows the woods better than I do and never lets me forget it. She is usually right, which is the worst of it.","pt":"There is a lumber camp out on the forest road. A man called Hob worked it. He had a brother. Read the board at the old mine; do not ask me.","nt":"I split logs for a living. You are standing in my house. Work it out.","b1":"Ash or oak?","b2":"Dry wood! Seasoned!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"laurence","name":"Laurence Byrd","age":48,"voice":"M","home":"in.02","trade":"cooper","kin":"husband of Felice, father of Joyce, brother of Randal","workWith":"casks for Greta and Goda","likes":"thinks Randal wastes himself on boats","work":"in.02","lines":{"g":"A cask is thirty staves that hate each other, held by two hoops. That is all there is to know. It takes seven years to learn.","g2":"Greta wanted a cask moved in her cellar the week before they found him. Then she did not. I thought it odd then.","me":"Laurence Byrd, cooper. The Hart’s ale, Goda’s beer, the salt fish: all in my oak.","nb":"My brother Randal builds boats on a lake with four boats on it. He had the hands to be a cooper.","pt":"Greta is waiting on malt from the mill before she can fill what I made her. Osric forgets. Somebody should jog him.","nt":"Joyce, the adze. No, the big one. Now, friend, which way is out? Show me.","b1":"Tight as a drum.","b2":"Joyce! Hoops!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"felice","name":"Felice Byrd","age":46,"voice":"W","home":"in.02","trade":"keeps the house, shaves hoops","kin":"Laurence’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"Maren cured her hands; she will hear nothing against her","work":"in.02","lines":{"g":"Come in. Mind the shavings. I would say I sweep them but nobody has ever caught up with a cooper.","g2":"I cried when I heard it. Silly. You do not know a thing holds your day together until it stops.","me":"Felice Byrd. I shave hoops and keep the peace between a cooper and his daughter, which is the harder craft.","nb":"Maren cured my hands when I could not hold a knife. People call her sour. I call her the reason I can work.","pt":"Old Maren has more than tonics in that cottage. She read the dead man’s tally when nobody else could. If you find a thing you cannot read, there is your door.","nt":"Laurence! ... You have picked a house where everyone owns a blade with a handle at each end.","b1":"Mind the shavings.","b2":"Lovely morning for it."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"joyce","name":"Joyce Byrd","age":20,"voice":"YW","home":"in.02","trade":"cooper’s daughter, as good as an apprentice","kin":"their daughter","workWith":"with her father","likes":"wants the trade in her own name","work":"in.02","lines":{"g":"That one is mine. Raised it, hooped it, tight first time. Father will tell you he helped.","g2":"Everything in this town has changed but the sign over our door. I am working on that too.","me":"Joyce Byrd. I have done seven years at the block the same as any apprentice. I want \"and Daughter\" on the board.","nb":"Old Wenn at the boatyard says a woman built the best boat that was ever on Mirrormere. He will not say who. I think it was his wife.","pt":"The doors under the quay are the boat-houses, and Old Wenn has the keys. He will tell you a whole story before he tells you no.","nt":"I can swing a cooper’s adze all day. I can certainly swing it once.","b1":"And Daughter. You will see.","b2":"Tight first time."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"randal","name":"Randal Byrd","age":43,"voice":"M","home":"in.113","trade":"boatwright and carpenter","kin":"Laurence’s brother","workWith":"boats for Finn and Peter","likes":"devoted to Old Wenn, who taught him","work":"in.113","lines":{"g":"Do not lean on her. She is green wood and steam and she is still deciding what shape to be.","g2":"They sent for me to look at the wharf. No boat brought that man. No keel mark, no oar. He walked out of the water.","me":"Randal Byrd. I build boats. My brother makes barrels, which are boats that gave up.","nb":"Old Wenn taught me everything and still tells me I plank like a farmer. I would not trade him for a new shed.","pt":"Finn Reedwater is at the end of the dock from first light. He found the man. He will tell it, if you ask kindly.","nt":"The tar pot is still warm. I would rather not waste it on you.","b1":"Mind the strakes.","b2":"Fair wind on the lake."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"wenn","name":"Old Wenn","age":76,"voice":"OM","home":"in.113","trade":"master boatwright, retired; holds the keys","kin":"none living","workWith":"with Randal","likes":"remembers when the lake had twenty boats","work":"in.113","lines":{"g":"Twenty boats. There were twenty on this water when I had my hair. Now sit down, I am going to tell you about each one.","g2":"I have seen what Mirrormere gives back. It gives back the drowned. That one, it did not want.","me":"Wenn. Just Wenn. I built boats for sixty years and now I hold the keys and the opinions.","nb":"Randal is the best pupil I ever had and I will tell him so on my deathbed and not one hour before.","pt":"The keys to the boat-houses are with me, and there they stay. Read the board on the wharf if you want to know how high the lake once stood.","nt":"Eh? Is it morning? No. Then you are a thief, and I am too old to chase you. Shut the door on your way out.","b1":"Twenty boats!","b2":"Planks like a farmer."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"barnaby","name":"Barnaby Crane","age":56,"voice":"M","home":"in.20","trade":"chandler: candles, cord, tar, lamp oil","kin":"husband of Ursel","workWith":"wax from Tamsin, rope from Ulric","likes":"sold the Elder candles \"for the cave\" and never asked","work":"in.20","lines":{"g":"Candles, cord, tar, tallow and lamp oil. Light and rope: there is nothing else a person truly needs.","g2":"The Elder bought a dozen candles off me every month, \"for the cave\". I took it for a manner of speaking.","me":"Barnaby Crane, chandler. Tamsin’s wax, Ulric’s cord, my wicks.","nb":"Ursel says I should have asked the Elder which cave. Ursel says a good many things after the event.","pt":"The gate is short of lamp oil and there is a notice for it. Marta Quill has the flasks.","nt":"I can lay my hand on a candle in the dark faster than you can find the door. Shall we see?","b1":"Tallow or wax?","b2":"Long evenings coming. Stock up."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"ursel","name":"Ursel Crane","age":54,"voice":"W","home":"in.20","trade":"the counter","kin":"Barnaby’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"asks what Barnaby will not","work":"in.20","lines":{"g":"He will tell you light and rope are all a person needs. Ask him who cooks.","g2":"A dozen candles a month for a cave, and my husband never once said \"which cave, Elder?\" Thirty years I have been married to that.","me":"Ursel Crane. I keep the counter. I also ask the questions, since somebody must.","nb":"Mercy Garrick sweeps the widow’s step across the way, every week, a house nobody has lived in for years. Ask yourself why.","pt":"Widow Hale’s house is locked and has been since she died. Somebody has the key. It is not the Warden; I asked.","nt":"Barnaby. Light a candle. We have enough of them. I want to see this person’s face.","b1":"Which cave, though?","b2":"Mind the tar."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"silas","name":"Silas Garrow","age":49,"voice":"M","home":"in.36","trade":"mason; cuts the headstones","kin":"none","workWith":"works for the chapel and Gilbert","likes":"quiet; drinks with Finn","work":"in.36","lines":{"g":"Hm. ... Stone does not talk either. It is why I like it.","g2":"The Brother has asked me for a headstone and given me no name to cut. I have left it blank. It will not be the first.","me":"Garrow. Mason. Walls, hearths, and every stone in the churchyard since my father’s.","nb":"Finn and I drink at the same table at the Hart and say nothing. It suits us both.","pt":"The north wall has a stair and a walk. I built the steps. From the top you can see the old tower on its crag.","nt":"I lift stone for a living. Go.","b1":"Hm.","b2":"Good stone, that."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"humphrey","name":"Humphrey Slate","age":47,"voice":"M","home":"in.111","trade":"butcher","kin":"husband of Hester","workWith":"beasts from Edda and Warin; hides to Fulk","likes":"loud; at odds with Hale over the shambles in the lane","work":"in.111","lines":{"g":"Mutton, pork when there is pork, and sausage you should not ask about. What will you have?","g2":"I helped carry him. I have carried sides of beef that were more alive. Dry, he was. Dry as jerky.","me":"Humphrey Slate, butcher. Edda’s beasts, Warin’s sheep, my knife. Hester keeps the till so there is some left.","nb":"Hale has had three words with me about blood in the lane. Where does he think meat comes from? A tree?","pt":"The watch pays for outlaws put down on the passes. I would go myself, but who would cut the chops?","nt":"Hester! Cleaver! ... No, I have it. Friend, I joint a hog in the time it takes you to reach that door.","b1":"Fresh today! Mostly!","b2":"Do not ask about the sausage."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"hester","name":"Hester Slate","age":44,"voice":"W","home":"in.111","trade":"the block and the till","kin":"Humphrey’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"Avice Hobb’s oldest friend","work":"in.111","lines":{"g":"He shouts. He always shouts. The meat is good and the weights are honest, that is what matters.","g2":"I saw them bring him up the lane. I have told Avice and nobody else, and Avice has told the town.","me":"Hester Slate. I keep the block clean and the till closed.","nb":"Avice Hobb and I were girls together. She married bread and I married meat, and we meet in the middle every market day.","pt":"Ellen Marsh smokes fish down by the water. If you want lake trout, hers is the best.","nt":"Humphrey has the cleaver. I have the other cleaver. There are always two.","b1":"Honest weight, every time.","b2":"Avice! A word!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"goda","name":"Goda Malkin","age":51,"voice":"W","home":"in.115","trade":"alewife: small beer for the wharf","kin":"mother of Bridget, widow","workWith":"casks from Laurence, malt from Osric","likes":"says Greta’s ale is honey and airs","work":"in.115","lines":{"g":"The bush is out, so the beer is ready. A farthing a jug and it will not put honey on your tongue or airs in your head.","g2":"Rings like it never stopped. I will say this for the Hart: Greta sent a cask round the night it came back, for everyone.","me":"Goda Malkin. I brew for people who work. Greta brews for people who sit.","nb":"Greta Barleybrook and I have not had a civil word in ten years and I would trust her with my last penny.","pt":"Osric at the mill owes half the town malt. If you are passing, tell him Goda remembers, and Greta is first in the line.","nt":"Bridget! The mash paddle! ... It is six feet of oak, stranger. I stir with it daily.","b1":"Bush is out!","b2":"Farthing a jug."},"when":"bell"},
  {"id":"bridget","name":"Bridget Malkin","age":23,"voice":"YW","home":"in.115","trade":"brews with her mother","kin":"Goda’s daughter","workWith":"with her","likes":"lets Jocelyn court her; has not decided","work":"in.115","lines":{"g":"Mother will tell you Greta’s ale is honey and airs. I drink at the Hart on Fridays. Do not tell her.","g2":"People want beer when things change and beer when they do not. It is a steady trade, at least.","me":"Bridget Malkin. I do the brewing now, mostly. Mother does the selling and the feud.","nb":"The tailor, Jocelyn. He measures me with his eyes every time I pass. I could do worse. I am checking whether I could do better.","pt":"Lark plays at the Hart every evening. He says he knows nine songs and seven of them are sad. Ask him for one.","nt":"Mother, do not get up, I have it. You. Out, before she finds the paddle.","b1":"Small beer, fresh.","b2":"Friday. Do not tell her."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"adda","name":"Adda the Rushworker","age":58,"voice":"OW","home":"in.51","trade":"basketmaker","kin":"a brother who went into the hills (the hermit)","workWith":"rushes from the reeds; baskets to everyone","likes":"will not hear him called mad","work":"in.51","lines":{"g":"Rush for seats, willow for carrying. Do not sit on the willow.","g2":"A man comes out of the dark alone and they cover him with a sail. My brother went into the hills alone. I think of it.","me":"Adda. They call me the Rushworker. I cut my own reeds on the north shore and I have the hands to prove it.","nb":"My brother lives up by the fall, by himself. People say hermit and mean mad. He is neither. He is tired of people, which is sense.","pt":"Follow the stream up past the old tower and you come to a pool under a fall. If a man is there, let him be. He is my brother, and he is not mad.","nt":"I weave by touch, so I do not need the lamp to find you. Out you go.","b1":"Rushes, fresh cut.","b2":"Not on the willow!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"peter","name":"Peter Marsh","age":37,"voice":"M","home":"in.38","trade":"boatman; carries on the lake","kin":"husband of Ellen, father of Will","workWith":"boat from Randal","likes":"saw the man on the shingle before Finn did and walked on","work":"in.38","lines":{"g":"The boat is not for hire today, the caulking is wet. And I do not go to the west shore, wet or dry.","g2":"Finn found him. Everyone says so. Finn found him. That is right.","me":"Peter Marsh. I row what needs rowing. My father did, his father did. Marsh House has always had oars by the door.","nb":"Finn Reedwater is on that dock before I am up and after I am in. I do not know when the man sleeps.","pt":"West shore, past the reeds, there is a cleft in the rock by the water. I have seen a light in it before dawn. I rowed the other way.","nt":"Ellen, take Will upstairs. ... I have an oar. I know it is a foolish thing to hold indoors. I am holding it.","b1":"Penny across.","b2":"Not the west shore."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"ellen","name":"Ellen Marsh","age":35,"voice":"W","home":"in.38","trade":"nets, smoking fish","kin":"Peter’s wife","workWith":"sells to Humphrey’s stall","likes":"knows Peter is hiding something","work":"in.38","lines":{"g":"Mind the rack, it is hot. Trout and eel, done over alder. Humphrey’s stall, or here if you ask nicely.","g2":"Peter came home grey that morning, before anyone had cried the news. He says he saw nothing. I have been married eleven years.","me":"Ellen Marsh. He catches the fares, I catch the supper.","nb":"Mercy Garrick mends our nets and will not take full price. Her brother is on the passes with the outlaws; she pays for him in knots.","pt":"Peter knows every yard of the lake shore. Ask him about the west side and watch him find something to do with his hands.","nt":"Peter. Wake up. Take the oar. Yes, the oar.","b1":"Smoked trout!","b2":"Will! Off the dock!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"will","name":"Will Marsh","age":8,"voice":"B","home":"in.38","trade":"school; minds the smoke rack","kin":"their son","workWith":"none","likes":"follows Finn about","work":"in.38","lines":{"g":"Finn says I am not to talk when the float is moving. Is the float moving? I am not allowed to look.","me":"I am Will. I am eight. I can row if someone else holds one of the oars.","nb":"Finn caught a pike as long as me. He let me carry the tail end.","pt":"There is a dead man under a sail on the wharf. I am not allowed to look at that either.","b1":"Is it moving?","b2":"I did not touch it!"}},
  {"id":"mercy","name":"Mercy Garrick","age":42,"voice":"W","home":"in.60","trade":"net mender","kin":"sister of Garrick One-Ear","workWith":"nets for Finn and Peter","likes":"sweeps Widow Hale’s step; still leaves a light for her brother","work":"in.60","lines":{"g":"Talk if you like. I can mend and listen. I cannot mend and be looked at.","g2":"The roads will be safer or they will not. Either way there is a man out there with one ear who is still my brother.","me":"Mercy Garrick. Yes. That Garrick. He was a good boy and a bad man, and I knew him as both.","nb":"The widow Hale was kind to me when this whole lane was not. So I sweep her step. I do not need a better reason.","pt":"There is a notice at the Hart that pays for outlaws. I will not ask you not to. I will ask you to tell me, after, if one of them had one ear.","nt":"I leave a light in that window for someone and it is not you. Out.","b1":"Mind the nets.","b2":"Knots hold. People do not."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"alaric","name":"Alaric Mossop","age":38,"voice":"M","home":"in.34","trade":"apothecary, two years in the town","kin":"none here","workWith":"buys jars from Beatrix","likes":"sure Maren is a hedge-witch; she outsells him","work":"shop.n2","lines":{"g":"Good day. Humours out of balance, I can see it from here. A touch choleric. I have a tincture.","g2":"Dry lungs, they tell me, and the herb-woman is \"reading\" him. Reading. I offered a proper examination. I was not answered.","me":"Alaric Mossop, apothecary, late of the lowland towns. Two years here. They still call me the new man.","nb":"Old Maren sells weeds in a twist of rag and half the town swears by her. I have glass jars. I have labels.","pt":"The woman Maren sends people up the slopes for frostcaps and pays them in tonic. I call that hedge-craft. You may call it what you like.","nt":"I am a lodger here, I own nothing but books and bottles. Take a bottle. I will not tell you which ones are poison.","b1":"Choleric. Decidedly.","b2":"I have a tincture for that."},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"wilmot","name":"Wilmot Arden","age":45,"voice":"W","home":"in.21","trade":"dyer","kin":"mother of Tom, widow","workWith":"dyes Hilda’s cloth for Anselm","likes":"Hilda’s closest friend","work":"in.21","lines":{"g":"Do not shake my hand unless you want to be blue till Candlemas.","g2":"Hilda came up that night and we sat by the vats till it was light. Neither of us wanted to be the one to say it.","me":"Wilmot Arden. Madder, weld and woad. Red, yellow and blue, and every colour you have seen in this town on a back.","nb":"Hilda weaves it and I colour it and Lindqvist sells it for three times what he gives the pair of us.","pt":"Hilda is at her loom by the river. She and the smith have a quarrel over cloth that neither of them will let lie.","nt":"That vat has stood a week. It stains for life. Take one more step and I will tip you in.","b1":"Mind the blue!","b2":"Tom! Stir, do not splash!"},"when":"body"},
  {"id":"tom","name":"Tom Arden","age":13,"voice":"B","home":"in.21","trade":"stirs the vats; school","kin":"Wilmot’s son","workWith":"with her","likes":"blue to the elbows; Tobin’s friend","work":"in.21","lines":{"g":"It does not wash off. I have tried. I have tried sand.","me":"Tom Arden. I stir. Red on the first day of the week, blue for the rest of it.","nb":"Tobin from the forge showed me the old tower. We were knights. He left something up there and now he is in trouble.","pt":"You can get up on the Hart’s gallery by the outside stair. Nobody stops you. You can see who is at the well.","b1":"It does not wash off!","b2":"I am stirring, I am stirring."}},
  {"id":"jordan","name":"Jordan Wold","age":58,"voice":"OM","home":"high.a","trade":"Reeve of Highfold","kin":"husband of Basilia, father of Kester","workWith":"answers to Rowan","likes":"holds that Highfold feeds Brackenfell and gets no thanks","work":"high.a","lines":{"g":"Reeve Wold. You are on Highfold ground now, and welcome, so long as the gates are shut behind you.","g2":"We heard it up here a day late, as we hear everything. Brackenfell does its deciding and Highfold is told.","me":"I answer for Highfold to the Elder: the flock, the tally and the toll. Six houses and more sheep than Brackenfell has people.","nb":"Rowan has not climbed this hill in four years. I go down to him. That tells you how it stands between the two places.","pt":"There is a toll ford on the west road, the far side of Brackenfell, when the guard is at his post. He has been at my son’s wedding for three weeks, in spirit.","nt":"The Reeve’s door is never barred, by custom. It has never been needed. Do not be the reason I change it.","b1":"Shut the gate behind you.","b2":"Count them twice."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"basilia","name":"Basilia Wold","age":55,"voice":"OW","home":"high.a","trade":"keeps the Reeve’s house","kin":"Jordan’s wife","workWith":"cheese to the market","likes":"still clearing up after the wedding","work":"high.a","lines":{"g":"Come in and eat something. There is still wedding cake. There will be wedding cake at Candlemas.","g2":"Down there they have their troubles. Up here I have forty borrowed trenchers to return and nobody will say whose are whose.","me":"Basilia Wold. I fed the whole hill for three days when Kester married, and I am still finding guests in the hay.","nb":"Rosalind is a town girl and thinks I do not see her looking down the valley of an evening. I looked the same way once.","pt":"If you are going down to Brackenfell, look for the stall with the blue cloth by the market cross. My cheese goes there, and it is not to be cut thin.","nt":"Another one sleeping it off? The hay is in the barn, the door is that way, and the wedding is over.","b1":"Have you eaten?","b2":"Whose trencher is this?"},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"kester","name":"Kester Wold","age":29,"voice":"YM","home":"high.c","trade":"shepherd","kin":"their son, husband of Rosalind","workWith":"the high flock","likes":"married three weeks","work":"high.c","lines":{"g":"Married three weeks! Did you know? Everyone knows. I keep telling them anyway.","g2":"Whatever they dug up or let out down there, it is a long way from my flock, and I have a wife to come home to.","me":"Kester Wold. The high flock is mine. So is the house, and so, God help her, is Rosalind.","nb":"Her uncle Warin keeps the low pasture. He looked me over like a ram at market before he would give her away.","pt":"The standing stones are north of us, a long walk up the valley. The flock will not graze near them. I do not ask the flock why.","nt":"Ros, stay there. Friend, I have had three weeks of people walking into this house with gifts. You have no gift.","b1":"Three weeks!","b2":"Come by, come by. Good dog."},"when":"end"},
  {"id":"rosalind","name":"Rosalind Wold","age":25,"voice":"YW","home":"high.c","trade":"from Brackenfell: Warin Shaw’s niece","kin":"Kester’s wife","workWith":"with him","likes":"misses the town and will not say so","work":"high.c","lines":{"g":"You have come up from the town? Is the market on? Was Tilly at the stall? Tell me anything.","g2":"I heard it. All the way up here, on the wind. I stood in the yard till it stopped.","me":"Rosalind Wold. Shaw, that was, a month ago. I can see the Hart’s roof from the top field if the air is clear.","nb":"My uncle Warin will not come up to visit. Says the hill is all sheep. He keeps sheep. I do not understand men.","pt":"If you go back down, tell Sabina at the draper’s the blue held in the wash. She will know what it means.","nt":"Kester. Kester! ... He will be a moment, he sleeps like a ewe. You should use the moment.","b1":"Is that the Hart’s smoke?","b2":"Morning! Any news from below?"},"when":"bell"}
];
